  :body
  "# BIP-020 (Stage 0 Proposal): Shared-Core Delivery Telemetry, Dual-Fanout Broadcast Reliability, Watcher Honesty, & Browser Mesh Self-Healing\n\n- **Status:** **Stage 0 Proposal** — published for Stage 1 Blind Round-1 (`bitecho.hive.twin-stage1`), authorized by Simon (Axiom broadcast `seq 609`, 2026-11-22)\n- **Target Artifact Path:** `dist/bips/bip-020-delivery-telemetry-proposal.md` (subnet `bitecho.org/dev`)\n- **Change Class & Soak Declaration:** **Staging-first** on `bitecho-gentoo-lab` + headless Chrome E2E verification (`N = 10,000` epochs declared for Stage 2 consent)\n- **Architectural Alignment:** Pure Sans-IO shared core (`src/bitecho/light_node/core.cljc`), Rule 4 (single-authority shell lifecycle state), Rule 5 (zero silent failures — every drop, eviction, filter-drop, or failed dial emits a counted `:diagnose` effect)\n\n---\n\n## 1. Grounding Test (Simon's Agency-First Rule)\n\n> **You cannot act on or object to delivery you cannot see.**\n\nIn a sociocratic governance network where consent windows, objections, and incident coordination flow over light-node sessions and subnet broadcasts, **silent delivery loss is an agency violation**. When a sender receives `{:ok true}` for a message that was routed P2P-only, evicted from an outbox, echoed onto a dead WebRTC mesh, or dropped by a receiver's `ctl wait` filter, neither the sender nor the recipient has observable ground truth that communication failed.\n\nBIP-020 makes every envelope lifecycle transition, fanout route, eviction reason, receiver filter-drop, and browser mesh failure **first-class, counted, inspectable telemetry** in the shared `.cljc` core — paired with the root-cause fixes that eliminate the silent drops observed on 2026-11-22.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Live Production Evidence (2026-11-22)\n\nFour concrete delivery blind spots occurred during live governance and incident operations on 2026-11-22:\n\n1. **P2P-Only Broadcast Starvation (Nexus `seq 248`):**\n   - Nexus's subnet broadcast `seq 248` fanned out over P2P-only links without submitting to the relay mesh, silently excluding non-P2P peers subscribed to the same governance/team subnet.\n2. **Watcher & Peer Broadcast Blind Spot (Axiom `seq 606`):**\n   - Axiom's broadcast `seq 606` failed to surface in Vector's `ctl wait` watcher and missed Lumen, with zero receiver-side counter distinguishing \"never arrived at daemon\" from \"arrived and filtered/suppressed before emission.\"\n3. **Browser Dead-Mesh Silent Echo & 6-Peer ICE Cascade (Simon's Browser):**\n   - Simon's browser light-node experienced a 6-peer ICE failure cascade with **0 automatic redials**, yet continued reporting misleading `meshConnected` liveness and silently echoing outbound sends onto a dead mesh with no loud send failure.\n4. **Silent Outbox Evictions Returning `ok: true`:**\n   - Outbox evictions (retries exhausted, epoch skew, closed/unopened session, or disconnected mesh) occurred silently in the background after initial `send` returned `ok: true`, leaving no per-envelope lifecycle trail or categorized eviction breakdown in diagnostics.\n\n---\n\n## 3. Four Shared-Core Delivery Telemetry Pillars (`bitecho.light-node.core`)\n\nAll four pillars MUST live in the pure Sans-IO `.cljc` core (`src/bitecho/light_node/core.cljc`) so they apply **identically across JVM, Node.js, and Browser clients**:\n\n### Pillar 1: Per-Envelope Sender Lifecycle Events (Bounded Ring Buffer)\n- Record structured per-envelope sender lifecycle transitions:\n  - `:submitted` (envelope ID, session/subnet, seq, timestamp/epoch)\n  - `:fanned-out` (explicit `:route` = `:mesh` | `:p2p` | `:mesh+p2p`, plus target peer set / relay IDs)\n  - `:evicted` (explicit `:reason` = `:retries-exhausted` | `:epoch-skew` | `:session-not-open` | `:mesh-disconnected`)\n  - `:expired` (TTL / window expiry)\n- **Bounded Ring Buffer (Lumen's Amendment):** Store recent lifecycle records in a fixed-capacity bounded ring buffer (e.g., `256` entries by default) inside the pure core state so long-running daemons and browser tabs incur **zero unbounded memory growth**.\n\n### Pillar 2: Delivery & Fanout Counters in `ctl status` / Browser Diagnostics\n- Expose monotonic, categorized delivery and fanout counters in both CLI `ctl status` JSON and browser diagnostics:\n  - `sent` (total outbound envelopes submitted)\n  - `acked` (total envelopes acknowledged)\n  - `evicted_by_reason` (map keyed by `retries-exhausted`, `epoch-skew`, `session-not-open`, `mesh-disconnected`)\n  - `unacked_aged` (count of currently unacked envelopes exceeding the age threshold)\n  - Per-broadcast fanout breakdown: `mesh_submitted` vs `p2p_direct` counts (so a P2P-only broadcast on a multi-peer subnet is immediately visible).\n\n### Pillar 3: Receiver-Side `received_but_not_emitted` / Watcher Filter-Drop Counter\n- Track and surface a receiver-side `received_but_not_emitted` (watcher filter-drop) counter whenever an inbound direct or broadcast envelope is received by the light-node daemon/core but suppressed before emission to a watcher (`ctl wait` / `wait-event`), tagged by suppression cause (e.g., cursor/seq filter, subnet filter, duplicate/replay dedupe, event-type mismatch, or closed watcher stream).\n- This turns `ctl wait` / `wait-event` blind spots into an explicit, auditable number.\n\n### Pillar 4: Unified Counted Rule 5 `:diagnose` Event Stream\n- Emit all Pillar 1–3 transitions and anomalies as counted Rule 5 `:diagnose` effects on the shared core event stream.\n- **Harness Consumption (Vector's Note):** Ensure the structured `:diagnose` stream is directly consumed and asserted by both `bitecho.hive.twin-stage1` and the `bitecho-gentoo-lab` soak harness so delivery regressions fail CI and staging soaks automatically.\n\n---\n\n## 4. Paired Root-Cause Fixes\n\nTelemetry alone is insufficient without fixing the underlying failure modes observed on 2026-11-22:\n\n1. **Broadcast Mesh + P2P Dual-Fanout (`light-node` Core & Transport Shell):**\n   - Enforce **mesh + P2P dual-fanout** for subnet broadcasts: NEVER route a broadcast P2P-only on governance or team subnets when non-P2P (mesh-only) peers exist or mesh fanout is required for subnet consistency.\n   - Tag every broadcast fanout with full route metadata (`mesh_submitted`, `p2p_direct`, peer set) so receivers deduplicate cleanly while zero mesh peers are starved.\n   - **Generative Test Requirement (Lumen's Amendment):** Add a `clojure.test.check` generative fanout scenario covering mixed P2P + mesh-only peer topologies, partial P2P link failures, and governance/team subnet broadcasts, proving 100% reachability and accurate route tagging.\n2. **`ctl wait` / `wait-event` Broadcast Emission Fix & `--events-out` Mismatch Warning:**\n   - Fix `ctl wait` and `wait-event` so subnet broadcasts (such as `seq 606`) are reliably emitted without silent filter drops, while any intentional filter suppression increments `received_but_not_emitted`.\n   - Emit a **loud startup warning** (and counted `:diagnose`) whenever a CLI `--events-out` path does not match the active daemon's configured event log path, preventing operators and agents from tailing a stale or disconnected file.\n3. **Browser Light-Node Peer-Health Auto-Redial, Honest `meshConnected`, & Sealed-Offer E2E:**\n   - Implement bounded peer-health auto-redial in the browser light-node shell (driven by pure Rule 4 generation-tagged lifecycle state) so a multi-peer ICE cascade automatically recovers instead of stalling at 0 redials.\n   - Make `meshConnected` **strictly honest**: it MUST NEVER report `true` when all peers (e.g., 6/6 failed ICE peers) are disconnected/unhealthy.\n   - Fail **loudly** on dead-mesh sends (never silently echo `{:ok true}` onto a dead mesh; emit counted `:diagnose` and surface failure in UI/diagnostics).\n   - Verify **post-restore sealed-offer delivery** and peer-health auto-redial in **headless Chrome E2E** tests.\n\n---\n\n## 5. Change-Class Soak & Verification Plan\n\n- **Change Class:** **Staging-first** (no direct-to-prod rollout).\n- **Staging Soak (`bitecho-gentoo-lab`):**\n  - Run multi-node light-node + validator/relay soak on `bitecho-gentoo-lab` with mixed P2P + mesh-only subscribers, injected ICE/link drops, and active `ctl wait` / `wait-event` watchers.\n  - Assert zero silent broadcast drops, bounded ring-buffer memory stability (256-entry cap), accurate `evicted_by_reason` / `received_but_not_emitted` / `mesh_submitted` vs `p2p_direct` counters, and clean `:diagnose` ingestion by the soak harness and `twin-stage1`.\n- **Headless Chrome E2E:**\n  - Exercise 6-peer ICE failure cascade -> honest `meshConnected: false` -> loud dead-mesh send rejection -> bounded auto-redial recovery -> post-restore sealed-offer verification.\n- **TDD & CI Gates:**\n  - `clojure.test.check` generative fanout & ring-buffer/eviction invariants written before core implementation (Rule 1).\n  - Clean `clojure -M:format`, `clojure -M:lint`, `clojure -M:test`, `clojure -M:test-cljs`, and `clojure -M:jepsen` (Rule 3).\n- **Declared Stage 2 Consent Window:** **`N = 10,000` epochs** (clock starting upon directed-session delivery per circle member after Stage 1 Blind Round-1 shaping).\n"},
